{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Difference of Cubes Identity.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Difference of Cubes Identity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"difference of cubes identity difference of cubes identity","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Difference of Cubes Identity.md","fileName":"Difference of Cubes Identity.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Difference of Cubes Identity","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Factoring and Algebraic Identities]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Factoring and Algebraic Identities","Cube of a Difference Identity","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Difference of Cubes Identity.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Difference of Cubes Identity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"difference of cubes identity difference of cubes identity","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Difference of Cubes Identity.md","fileName":"Difference of Cubes Identity.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Difference of Cubes Identity","heading":"Difference of Cubes Identity","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The difference of cubes identity is\n\n$$a^3-b^3=(a-b)(a^2+ab+b^2).$$\n\nThe sign in the linear factor matches the original difference, while every term in the quadratic factor is positive. Expanding the product cancels the mixed terms and leaves (a^3-b^3).\n\nFor example, (x^3-8=(x-2)(x^2+2x+4)). The identity is distinct from the [[Cube of a Difference Identity]]: (a^3-b^3) has two terms, whereas ((a-b)^3) expands to four.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Factoring and Algebraic Identities","Cube of a Difference Identity","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Difference of Cubes Identity.md::2","sourceKey":